---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, AUTHOR } from "../site.config.js";
import { breadcrumbSchema } from "../lib/schema.js";

const crumbs = [{ name: "Editorial Policy", path: "/editorial-policy/" }];
const schema = [breadcrumbSchema(crumbs)];
---

<Base
  title="Editorial & Mathematical Verification Policy"
  description="Our editorial principles, source attribution rules, and rigorous auditing process for payroll and labor calculation algorithms."
  schema={schema}
  crumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 lg:py-16">
    <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-8 sm:p-12 shadow-sm prose prose-slate max-w-none text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-brand-50 text-brand-700 border border-brand-200 not-prose mb-3">
        Integrity & Governance
      </span>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 not-prose">Editorial & Verification Policy</h1>
      
      <p class="lead text-base text-slate-600 mt-2">
        At {SITE.name}, accuracy in wage computations directly impacts household budgeting, contract negotiation, and statutory compliance. We uphold rigorous editorial standards to ensure every calculator and guide meets professional engineering benchmarks.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-8" />

      <h2>1. Primary Source Attribution</h2>
      <p>
        Every formula, deduction rate, and threshold implemented on this website originates from official, authoritative government sources:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>United States Code (Title 29 - Labor):</strong> Specifically Fair Labor Standards Act provisions (29 U.S.C. §§ 201-219).</li>
        <li><strong>Code of Federal Regulations (29 CFR Part 778):</strong> Overtime compensation guidelines and regular rate calculations.</li>
        <li><strong>Internal Revenue Service (IRS):</strong> Publications 15 (Circular E), 15-T, and annual Revenue Procedures defining inflation-adjusted brackets.</li>
        <li><strong>State Departments of Labor:</strong> Annual legislative enactments modifying local minimum wages and paid family leave withholding rates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. How Articles Are Produced and Checked</h2>
      <p>
        Articles are drafted with the help of AI writing tools and are edited by the author before publication. Nothing is published straight from a tool, and drafts that cannot be verified are held back instead of published.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each article is checked against the primary sources listed in its "Sources and further reading" section. Figures, thresholds, citations, and worked examples are compared with the statute, regulation, or agency publication, and example calculations are recomputed by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some figures, such as tax brackets, standard deductions, and the Social Security wage base, change every year. Where an example uses a figure from a particular year, the article says so, and readers should confirm the current value at the linked source.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Corrections & Feedback</h2>
      <p>
        If you find an error, a conflict with an official source, or a figure that is out of date, please write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. Reports are reviewed, verified errors are corrected, and the article shows the date it was last updated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
